WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:07.089
Gravitational waves are ripples in the fabric
of spacetime, predicted by Einstein’s laws

00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:11.550
of general relativity, but they are incredibly
difficult to detect.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:17.000
To see them you need a detector that can accurately
measure distances 10,000 times smaller than

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.009
a proton.

00:00:20.009 --> 00:00:21.009
Thats crazy!

00:00:21.009 --> 00:00:24.820
That’s like trying to measure the distance
from our Sun to the nearest star to accuracy

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.320
of the width human hair.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.810
But we have a technology on Earth that can
do that: aLIGO the Advanced Laser Interferometer

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:39.750
Gravitational-wave Observatory, and back in
November 2015, on a Monday morning, LIGO detected

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.410
the first gravitational wave that humans have
ever directly observed.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:50.350
Where they came from and what this means for
space science is nothing short of mind blowing!

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.221
This is the story.

00:00:52.221 --> 00:00:56.130
A long long time ago, in a galaxy far far
away.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.899
I’m not kidding, 1.3 billion years ago and
1.3 billion light years away, two black holes

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:06.820
were stuck in a perilous orbit around one
another getting closer and closer.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:11.700
Black holes are incredible objects, the pull
of their gravity - the amount they bend spacetime

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.400
- is so strong that no light can escape them.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.750
No one knows what exists in the centre of
a black hole as normal physics completely

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.430
breaks down.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.479
What we do know is that they are infinitely
dense.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:28.990
One of these orbiting black holes was 29 times
the mass of the Sun and the other was 36 times

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:33.890
the mass of the Sun, but they were only about
200km wide.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.700
Which is tiny in comparison to the Sun which
is over a million kilometres wide!

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.420
And these black holes were orbiting each other
really really fast, about the same frequency

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.580
as the blade on a blender.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.009
Imagine that, such massive objects rotating
so quickly.

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:53.960
These orbiting masses created ripples in the
fabric of spacetime called gravitational waves,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:58.170
and the closer they orbited the bigger these
waves got, until the black holes collided

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.829
at half of the speed of light.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:04.610
And when they merged they formed a new black
hole that rang kind of like a bell, throwing

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:10.580
out colossal amounts of energy as gravitational
waves until it settled into a perfect sphere.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.670
And all of this happened in 0.2 seconds.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.750
And in the collision, they turned a huge amount
of mass into gravitational wave energy.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:23.980
They lost a mass equal to three times the
mass of the Sun which got turned in to gravitational

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.650
wave energy by Einsteins equation E=mc^2.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.090
This created a huge wake of gravitational
waves that ripped out in all directions at

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.400
the speed of light.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.209
And, and this is the thing that gets me, over
that last fifth of a second this collision

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:46.879
released more than ten times more energy than
total output of all of the stars in the entire

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.090
rest of the Universe!

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:55.110
It just completely boggles the mind!

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.909
Meanwhile on Earth…

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:02.250
At this exact time our planet was looking
very different to what we see now.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:08.930
It was a barren wasteland, there was no grass
or trees, in fact no plants or animals at

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.290
all.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.630
Life at this stage had only come as far as
microscopic multicellular creatures that lived

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:15.630
in the sea.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:20.930
And while the gravitational waves tore through
space towards us all of the complex life on

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:26.940
Earth evolved and grew: plants and animals
developed, amphibians crawled on land, there

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.959
was extinctions, reptiles and dinosaurs and
mammals, more extinctions.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:40.290
Primates evolved into all of human civilization
right up until Saturday 12th November 2015

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.040
when the scientists at LIGO turned it on to
begin their initial tests.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:50.470
A mere two days later and … boop … the
gravitational waves flew past us and the first

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.730
direct detection on Earth was made.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:59.459
And that sound … boop … is actually what
these waves sounded like.

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:03.730
Even though gravitational-waves are ripples
in spacetime and not ripples in the air, they

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:07.989
vibrate at similar frequencies, so we can
actually turn them into sound waves and listen

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:13.980
to them … boop … It might not sound very
impressive, but detection of gravitational

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.700
waves means a huge amount for science.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.760
The results of this detection have already
been profound.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.580
This is the very first time that black holes
have been directly detected, in fact gravitational

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.390
waves are the only way you can directly detect
them!

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:32.410
Consider this: all of our knowledge about
the Universe to this date has come to us through

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:38.670
telescopes that measure light from the electromagnetic
spectrum like radio waves, visible light,

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:43.240
x-rays, or through detectors that measure
subatomic particles.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.230
But all of these existing telescopes are completely
blind to gravitational waves.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:53.320
This means that gravitational wave astronomy
is an entirely NEW THING, a whole new way

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.330
to observe the Universe.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:05:00.760
And it has already solved some important science
questions: gravitational waves exist, black

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.090
holes exist, and gravitational waves travel
at the speed of light.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:08.610
Here’s how LIGO detectors work.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:13.850
There are two L shaped buildings, one in Hanford
Washington, and another in Livingstone Louisiana

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:20.200
make up LIGO and also there’s another detector
called VIRGO which is in Italy near Pisa.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:24.250
Having several detectors on Earth means that
scientists can verify a signal from space

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:29.100
by seeing if it appears at all of the different
detectors, and they are far apart because

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.910
it means they can see roughly in what direction
the wave is coming from by triangulating the

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:35.910
signal.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.880
Even with these waves travelling at light
speed there is a delay of a few milliseconds

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.990
between each of the detectors.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.530
Each of these L shaped buildings houses a
thing called a laser interferometer where

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:51.570
a laser is produced, then split into two beams
which each fly down a 4km long arm, and then

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:57.060
they are reflected off a 40kg pure silicon
test mass which is hung from the ceiling.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.750
It is hung from the ceiling on a series of
pendulums to insulate it from any outside

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:06.060
disturbances like trucks moving past, or people
sneezing.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.650
When you are trying to detect distances with
a sensitivity less than the nucleus of an

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.030
atom, getting rid of external noise is very
important.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:19.790
Each arm bounces the laser back and forth
400 times to make the arm effectively 1600km

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:25.560
long, and each time around the laser is boosted
to make it stronger.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.460
Then after its journey the light is collected
and recombined in such a way that if the arms

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:35.120
are the same length the light from each arm
cancels out producing no signal.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.940
But if a gravitational wave ripples past,
it will stretch or contract each of the arms

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.190
making the light from each arm arrive at the
detector at slightly different times.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:49.120
That means that when they are recombined,
they will no longer fully cancel out, and

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.350
a signal will appear.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.140
And this is exactly what happened on the 14th
November.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:59.060
It’s crazy to me to imagine that if they
had turned on this detector just two days

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:03.440
later, they would have completely missed the
signal that had been travelling towards us

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.620
for 1.3 billion years.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:10.490
This makes me wonder what other signals from
deep space have we already missed, and what

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:14.440
is still out there screaming towards us waiting
to be discovered that we just don’t know

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.440
about yet.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:19.650
In fact at the time that I made this video
LIGO has detected another even stronger black

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.800
hole collision, and we can expect a lot more
in the coming months and years.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.660
It has already achieved many of its science
goals, there are many more it is aiming for.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:31.150
For example it might be able to give us a
more accurate measure of how fast the universe

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.810
is expanding, and so how much dark energy
there is.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:39.330
It will hopefully be able to look at what
makes stars go supernova, and might be able

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:44.440
to probe the very nature of spacetime and
see if it is made of things called cosmic

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:45.440
strings.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.860
But the most exciting thing is that we don’t
know what it will find.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.650
This is one of the best parts of science,
when you’ve got a new tool to peer into

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.490
a realm of reality that you’ve never been
able to access before.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.110
Who knows what you’ll find?

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:03.080
Maybe you’ll discover things that help explain
some of the great mysteries of the Universe,

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.780
maybe we’ll find things that we can’t
explain at all, and then we have to come up

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.240
with new physics.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.220
In any case I find it super exciting and I
can’t wait to see more results.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:16.800
So there you go, those are the basics of gravitational
wave astronomy.

